on  westerns and kind of you know there was  a time when the comic-book movie you  know those and I would count myself  among them who feel like oh god is there  a night on television or I don't see a  comic-book character is there not is  there a weekend movie releases where I  won't see a comic book character there  was a time of 30 40 50 years ago where  I'm sure some similarly cantankerous  soul was going can I just turn on  Della's and not see a Western and I just  go to the movies and not see a Western  and what's happened is the Western the  shitty westerns have disappeared and the  great westerns have stayed and but still  I think people have a terrible  resistance to them because they see them  mainly as kind of varmint talk and  saloons and and that's all actually what  I mean I'm sure those there's a  wonderful writer of Bonanza in the  audience who I'm going to crush but it's  more what television did to the Western  than what the great what John Ford did  to the Western there isn't really much  varmint talk in in great American  western films that's something else  Pacha prior to this film Charles Xavier  as played by you had died on screen one  and a half times right like one yes once  maybe had you given up hope that you  would be asked to play this character  again or were you always like it's comic  books of course I'm coming back  well actors never give up hope yeah he  was obliterated by Jean Grey and it was  very uncomfortable no it actually was  very uncommon and then we assumed that  he must have died when those mechanical  monsters at the end of Days of Future  past finally broke into our central  control room there wasn't much hope left  there I think um but when the call came  hey is going to be a Wolverine movie and  they would like to talk to you about it  I was delighted because every part of  this  experience 17 years has been exciting  and stimulating and warm and funny and  crazy and then as word began to filter  down from the production that this was  going to be a different movie my  interest piqued and piqued and peeks and  then finally I went to talk to James and  a few minutes into the conversation he  learned I hadn't seen his script and you  did a lot of yelling I remember I was  impressed by the yelling you did bring  this quietly and I took it home and I  read it and what what excited me most  was not the scenes not the dialogue  initially but it was the stage direction  of the location where my first scene  took place because nothing in the past  of x-men had ever been set for me in  such a location it's so fun to see these  great scenes with you at Hugh and then  it's so wonderful to have this exciting  new addition of the character of Laura  played by Daphne Keane who I've been  researching because I'm just so  fascinated by her and I read that she is  the child of two actors her dad as a  British actor who's on the crown and  then her mom is a Spanish actor so how  did you find her when Scott and I  finished last pass on the script the we  had a very simple breakdown for what we  needed for the role of Laura which was  someone between 10 and 12 years old who  was an amazing actress physically  capable bilingual and of Latino descent  and the and so then the search went out  in South America Europe in the United  States obviously everywhere for this kid  you also couldn't have them read the  actual script because of the environment  we live in of secrecy and worry you have  to invent kind of this odd ten gentle  parallel universe audition piece for  them to do  which is really annoying for me and the  and but this tape came in one day from  London of young actress in Spain in  Madrid who would whose dad had recorded  this little two-minute iPhone movie with  her and I watch this thing and all I can  say is that I immediately knew there was  like two things new I knew it was her  she was going to be that I knew it was  just done but I also knew the movie  would be okay you know Scott and I had  written a three-legged stool essentially  and this guy's an incredible actor and I  made three movies two at that point with  Hugh Jackman and he's an incredible  actor so there's a certain amount of  stability and comfort you feel about  those two legs of the stool but then  this other creature was a complete  question mark and this tape just I can't  explain it other than to say that it  just it said I'm here and I can do it I  can't thank the four of you enough for  being here and for making a superhero  movie for grown-ups  congratulate welcome and well done
 

